# Masks for units that must not run inside a container. Each symlink points the
# unit name at /dev/null under /etc/systemd/system/, which is the mask location
# for administrator overrides. The unit files themselves live in /usr/lib/systemd
# and are owned by other packages; shipping a file at the same path would be a
# dpkg file conflict, so symlinks installed here instead.
#
# lxc-net.service: its own guard is ConditionVirtualization=!lxc, which matches
# only the lxc and lxc-libvirt virtualisation identifiers. Docker and Podman
# report their virtualisation identifier as docker and podman respectively, so
# ConditionVirtualization=!lxc evaluates to true inside Docker or Podman and the
# unit starts. When it does it brings up an lxcbr0 bridge and a dnsmasq instance
# that depend on iptables NAT the container does not own, causing the unit to
# fail in a way that can stall other units ordered against network.target.
#
# corosync.service: this image is single-node and ships no cluster
# configuration, so corosync has nothing to join. Started anyway it either
# fails outright or sits retrying, and it carries a hard
# Requires=network-online.target, which drags an ordering dependency into the
# boot for a service that cannot do anything useful here.
#
# An earlier revision justified this mask by claiming that masking
# systemd-networkd-wait-online.service leaves network-online.target permanently
# unreachable. That is wrong and worth stating plainly: masking a unit whose
# only job is to DELAY the target makes the target reachable sooner, not never.
# The mask is still correct, but for the reason above rather than that one.
#
# ConditionKernelCommandLine=!nocluster provides no escape either way, because
# /proc/cmdline inside a container is the host's command line, which is not a
# lever this image controls.
dev/null etc/systemd/system/watchdog-mux.service
dev/null etc/systemd/system/systemd-networkd-wait-online.service
dev/null etc/systemd/system/kbrequest.target
dev/null etc/systemd/system/lxc-net.service
dev/null etc/systemd/system/corosync.service

# The ifupdown2 binary at /usr/share/ifupdown2/ifupdown2 is shared by ifup,
# ifdown, ifreload and ifquery, and it dispatches on argv[0]: invoked under any
# other name it prints "error: Unexpected executable." and exits without doing
# anything.  /usr/sbin/ifreload is diverted to this package's wrapper and the
# real binary is renamed aside to /usr/sbin/ifreload.distrib -- a name the
# binary itself rejects.  This link gives the wrapper a path whose basename is
# ifreload, so `ifreload -V` reaches the real implementation and prints the
# version line PVE's assert_ifupdown2_installed() parses.  Without it that
# assertion fails and the whole network-apply path aborts before it starts.
usr/share/ifupdown2/ifupdown2 usr/libexec/pve-container-shim/ifupdown2/ifreload
